WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.437
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:05.948 --> 00:00:07.740
MIKHAIL CHRESTKHA:
Welcome to "Architecting

00:00:07.740 --> 00:00:09.030
with Google Cloud."

00:00:09.030 --> 00:00:11.130
Machine learning
continues to be applied

00:00:11.130 --> 00:00:13.300
to use cases across industries.

00:00:13.300 --> 00:00:16.620
The airlines and transportation
industry is no exception.

00:00:16.620 --> 00:00:19.470
I'm thrilled today to chat with
Ksenia Khlyustina, a machine

00:00:19.470 --> 00:00:21.840
learning platform
manager at FLYR Labs,

00:00:21.840 --> 00:00:24.060
where her team leverages
data and machine learning

00:00:24.060 --> 00:00:27.240
to help airlines better
forecast demand, set prices,

00:00:27.240 --> 00:00:28.620
and optimize revenue.

00:00:28.620 --> 00:00:31.950
I'm particularly excited to
also have Ksenia give us a peek

00:00:31.950 --> 00:00:35.250
into the core components of
FLYR Labs as ML platform.

00:00:35.250 --> 00:00:37.450
Welcome, Ksenia, and
thanks for joining today.

00:00:37.450 --> 00:00:40.030
Can you start off by telling
us a bit about yourself?

00:00:40.030 --> 00:00:41.030
KSENIA KHLYUSTINA: Sure.

00:00:41.030 --> 00:00:41.760
Hello.

00:00:41.760 --> 00:00:46.540
Yes, my name is Ksenia, and I've
been with FLYR for three years.

00:00:46.540 --> 00:00:48.780
So in general, I have
about eight years

00:00:48.780 --> 00:00:53.040
of experience in the
industry in multiple domains.

00:00:53.040 --> 00:00:57.900
But, however, I started
working in machine

00:00:57.900 --> 00:00:59.490
learning only three years ago.

00:00:59.490 --> 00:01:00.150
MIKHAIL CHRESTKHA:
And now, can you

00:01:00.150 --> 00:01:02.250
tell us a little about
your company, FLYR Labs?

00:01:02.250 --> 00:01:05.370
KSENIA KHLYUSTINA: FLYR Labs
is a company been bringing

00:01:05.370 --> 00:01:06.900
intelligence to airlines.

00:01:06.900 --> 00:01:10.200
So we are trying to
use machine learning

00:01:10.200 --> 00:01:15.120
and AI to be able to
predict demand and optimize

00:01:15.120 --> 00:01:16.500
the pricing for the airlines.

00:01:16.500 --> 00:01:19.830
So we want to make sure that
every flight, at takeoff,

00:01:19.830 --> 00:01:22.742
is 100% full and bringing the
maximum revenue to the airline.

00:01:22.742 --> 00:01:24.450
MIKHAIL CHRESTKHA:
Oh, wow, that's great.

00:01:24.450 --> 00:01:27.780
And that field is especially
particularly relevant

00:01:27.780 --> 00:01:30.360
and important today for
the airlines industry

00:01:30.360 --> 00:01:33.600
in the midst of the pandemic
and now how consumer behavior

00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:35.035
changes really, really fast.

00:01:35.035 --> 00:01:36.660
So now we are here
to talk a little bit

00:01:36.660 --> 00:01:37.618
about machine learning.

00:01:37.618 --> 00:01:41.070
Could you discuss some of the
top use cases ML is applied to?

00:01:41.070 --> 00:01:45.160
KSENIA KHLYUSTINA: We basically
have only one use case for now.

00:01:45.160 --> 00:01:47.700
So we are taking all
the historical data

00:01:47.700 --> 00:01:48.450
from the airline.

00:01:48.450 --> 00:01:51.570
We're taking their
competitors' information.

00:01:51.570 --> 00:01:54.480
We are taking information
about future events

00:01:54.480 --> 00:01:57.780
at the origin or
destination of the flight

00:01:57.780 --> 00:02:01.410
and putting this
all into our models

00:02:01.410 --> 00:02:04.770
and outputting the
demand forecasts, which

00:02:04.770 --> 00:02:07.950
are used to optimize the
pricing and deliver this

00:02:07.950 --> 00:02:11.070
to the airline that are lightly
used in their production

00:02:11.070 --> 00:02:11.850
systems.

00:02:11.850 --> 00:02:14.370
MIKHAIL CHRESTKHA: And before
we dive into that use case

00:02:14.370 --> 00:02:16.560
and how you've architected
a platform around it,

00:02:16.560 --> 00:02:19.170
I think I wanted to dig a
little bit deeper into your team

00:02:19.170 --> 00:02:23.410
and how it fits within the
broader FLYR Labs organization.

00:02:23.410 --> 00:02:24.820
I think a lot of
companies today,

00:02:24.820 --> 00:02:26.880
they might only have
one data science team.

00:02:26.880 --> 00:02:29.528
They struggle to
operationalize ML models.

00:02:29.528 --> 00:02:31.320
Could you talk a little
bit about your team

00:02:31.320 --> 00:02:33.870
and the other teams at FLYR
that are tackling this problem?

00:02:33.870 --> 00:02:36.060
KSENIA KHLYUSTINA:
Yeah, so FLYR is mostly

00:02:36.060 --> 00:02:37.230
built around the design.

00:02:37.230 --> 00:02:39.990
So of course, we have more
than one data science team.

00:02:39.990 --> 00:02:41.700
We have a data
science organization,

00:02:41.700 --> 00:02:43.120
which my team is a part of.

00:02:43.120 --> 00:02:44.820
So this data
science organization

00:02:44.820 --> 00:02:46.140
consists of three teams--

00:02:46.140 --> 00:02:48.840
data science team itself,
machine learning platform,

00:02:48.840 --> 00:02:51.300
and machine learning
infrastructure team.

00:02:53.910 --> 00:02:56.220
Besides this organization,
there is also

00:02:56.220 --> 00:02:59.430
validation team, which
is kind of participating

00:02:59.430 --> 00:03:03.570
in machine learning,
data science, as well.

00:03:03.570 --> 00:03:04.950
But they're a little bit apart.

00:03:04.950 --> 00:03:07.420
But I will talk
about them as well.

00:03:07.420 --> 00:03:12.300
So data science team is a
team responsible for basically

00:03:12.300 --> 00:03:15.000
building a model itself.

00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:20.250
They are pure data scientists,
trying not to touch any MLOps.

00:03:20.250 --> 00:03:22.080
Then machine
learning platform is

00:03:22.080 --> 00:03:26.220
a team that builds the
platform, as you can understand

00:03:26.220 --> 00:03:29.490
from the name, it builds a
platform around these models

00:03:29.490 --> 00:03:30.950
and machine learning.

00:03:30.950 --> 00:03:32.670
So this is the
platform that is used

00:03:32.670 --> 00:03:35.528
to run inference and
production environment and not

00:03:35.528 --> 00:03:37.320
only production
environment on daily basis,

00:03:37.320 --> 00:03:40.650
and deliver and basically use
the models on the production

00:03:40.650 --> 00:03:41.260
system.

00:03:41.260 --> 00:03:44.190
And then there is a machine
learning infrastructure team,

00:03:44.190 --> 00:03:48.110
which is focusing on
empowering data science team

00:03:48.110 --> 00:03:50.660
and machine learning platform
team to do their job.

00:03:50.660 --> 00:03:54.710
So they are mostly focusing
on tools around all of this.

00:03:54.710 --> 00:03:56.500
So that's how it looks.

00:03:56.500 --> 00:03:58.330
And then validation
team is a team

00:03:58.330 --> 00:04:05.830
that is responsible for
testing the models, let's say.

00:04:05.830 --> 00:04:08.410
They are trying to see how
the models are performing

00:04:08.410 --> 00:04:16.040
in real life or over many days
of being used in production.

00:04:16.040 --> 00:04:19.269
So they are trying to validate
how good our models basically

00:04:19.269 --> 00:04:19.870
are.

00:04:19.870 --> 00:04:20.200
MIKHAIL CHRESTKHA: Great.

00:04:20.200 --> 00:04:21.242
Yeah, thanks for sharing.

00:04:21.242 --> 00:04:23.770
I think it's great to see that
you're really at a maturity

00:04:23.770 --> 00:04:26.230
level where you have
different teams focused

00:04:26.230 --> 00:04:29.140
on different parts
of the lifecycle

00:04:29.140 --> 00:04:33.880
and really optimizing the time
to get models into production.

00:04:33.880 --> 00:04:35.590
And you really have
great delegation

00:04:35.590 --> 00:04:37.298
of duties across teams, here.

00:04:37.298 --> 00:04:39.340
So we are here, again, to
talk about architecting

00:04:39.340 --> 00:04:40.300
on Google Cloud.

00:04:40.300 --> 00:04:41.440
So maybe you could
give us a little bit

00:04:41.440 --> 00:04:43.540
of the story around why
you initially chose Google

00:04:43.540 --> 00:04:45.440
Cloud a number of years ago.

00:04:45.440 --> 00:04:48.040
KSENIA KHLYUSTINA: We made this
decision around six years ago,

00:04:48.040 --> 00:04:51.130
when we were still
a small startup.

00:04:51.130 --> 00:04:55.060
And one of the
main reasons for it

00:04:55.060 --> 00:04:59.560
was Google Bootcamp, Google
startup empowering program,

00:04:59.560 --> 00:05:01.630
where Google gave
us a credit and we

00:05:01.630 --> 00:05:05.710
were able to use Google
Cloud Platform basically

00:05:05.710 --> 00:05:07.520
for free for a while.

00:05:07.520 --> 00:05:11.680
This was quite a
big thing for us

00:05:11.680 --> 00:05:15.940
because we didn't have much
money at the time, let's say.

00:05:15.940 --> 00:05:18.400
But that was not
the only reason.

00:05:18.400 --> 00:05:22.420
The other reason was that
Google had the great support

00:05:22.420 --> 00:05:25.390
for Kubernetes, probably the
best at the time on the market.

00:05:25.390 --> 00:05:28.630
And we really wanted
to be able to use

00:05:28.630 --> 00:05:30.140
Kubernetes in our solution.

00:05:30.140 --> 00:05:33.550
So this is reason number two.

00:05:33.550 --> 00:05:35.230
Why are we staying with Google?

00:05:35.230 --> 00:05:36.610
What do we like about this?

00:05:36.610 --> 00:05:40.960
It's still the great Kubernetes
support and BigQuery,

00:05:40.960 --> 00:05:43.960
which BigQuery is--

00:05:43.960 --> 00:05:47.330
basically, our whole
company runs on BigQuery.

00:05:47.330 --> 00:05:49.870
So we are tightly bound to this.

00:05:49.870 --> 00:05:53.247
And so far it's been
working great for us.

00:05:53.247 --> 00:05:54.580
MIKHAIL CHRESTKHA: Yeah, thanks.

00:05:54.580 --> 00:05:55.872
Yeah, great to hear that story.

00:05:55.872 --> 00:05:58.840
I'm glad that FLYR Labs
is still on GCP, kind

00:05:58.840 --> 00:06:01.000
of growing with your business.

00:06:01.000 --> 00:06:03.760
And it feels like, really,
that big bet on BigQuery

00:06:03.760 --> 00:06:06.730
and Kubernetes has paid
off and is really part

00:06:06.730 --> 00:06:09.170
of your core product offering.

00:06:09.170 --> 00:06:12.560
So now let's get into
the ML platform itself.

00:06:12.560 --> 00:06:14.710
In addition to BigQuery
and Kubernetes,

00:06:14.710 --> 00:06:16.840
what are some of the
other GCP services that

00:06:16.840 --> 00:06:18.900
make up your ML platform today?

00:06:18.900 --> 00:06:19.900
KSENIA KHLYUSTINA: Sure.

00:06:19.900 --> 00:06:21.190
We're using Dataflow.

00:06:21.190 --> 00:06:22.540
We're using Vertex AI.

00:06:22.540 --> 00:06:24.572
We are using Kubeflow
and TensorFlow.

00:06:24.572 --> 00:06:25.780
MIKHAIL CHRESTKHA: OK, great.

00:06:25.780 --> 00:06:29.290
So you seem to have really
adopted some of the industry

00:06:29.290 --> 00:06:32.050
standards and really focused
on open source, which

00:06:32.050 --> 00:06:34.510
I think is a big core
component of Google Cloud.

00:06:34.510 --> 00:06:38.140
As well, one of
our main objectives

00:06:38.140 --> 00:06:40.730
is really to support
the community,

00:06:40.730 --> 00:06:42.800
especially in the
open-source space.

00:06:42.800 --> 00:06:46.600
So now, can you maybe talk about
the main components of your ML

00:06:46.600 --> 00:06:48.760
architecture at a
higher level, to start?

00:06:48.760 --> 00:06:49.780
KSENIA KHLYUSTINA: Sure.

00:06:49.780 --> 00:06:53.350
Three main components
are our three pipelines--

00:06:53.350 --> 00:06:55.300
training dataset
generation pipeline,

00:06:55.300 --> 00:06:58.075
model training pipeline, and
daily inference pipeline.

00:06:58.075 --> 00:06:59.450
MIKHAIL CHRESTKHA:
Great, thanks.

00:06:59.450 --> 00:07:02.863
Yeah, I think breaking it
down like that really makes

00:07:02.863 --> 00:07:04.030
things a little bit clearer.

00:07:04.030 --> 00:07:06.520
I think sometimes
architecting a broad ML

00:07:06.520 --> 00:07:09.370
system can feel intimidating
or complex for teams.

00:07:09.370 --> 00:07:11.890
But focusing on how data
flows through the system

00:07:11.890 --> 00:07:14.260
with your three-pipeline
design really makes

00:07:14.260 --> 00:07:16.610
it much more understandable.

00:07:16.610 --> 00:07:19.240
So can you start off with the
training dataset generation

00:07:19.240 --> 00:07:19.900
pipeline?

00:07:19.900 --> 00:07:21.108
KSENIA KHLYUSTINA: Of course.

00:07:21.108 --> 00:07:25.340
Training dataset generation
demands the most resources.

00:07:25.340 --> 00:07:29.530
So how it works-- it uses
Kubeflow as an orchestrator.

00:07:29.530 --> 00:07:31.450
It pulls data from BigQuery.

00:07:31.450 --> 00:07:35.950
It's usually a few years
of airline historical data.

00:07:35.950 --> 00:07:40.000
And then it runs on Dataflow
using beam programming model.

00:07:40.000 --> 00:07:42.580
As an output, it
generates TFRecords,

00:07:42.580 --> 00:07:46.570
that we later put in
Google Cloud Storage

00:07:46.570 --> 00:07:50.800
and register in our
model metadata store.

00:07:50.800 --> 00:07:53.890
So this pipeline
is very demanding.

00:07:53.890 --> 00:07:56.590
That's why we run
it on Google TPU.

00:07:56.590 --> 00:08:00.040
And even though
TPU is very fast,

00:08:00.040 --> 00:08:03.970
it still takes hours to generate
such a training dataset.

00:08:03.970 --> 00:08:06.640
MIKHAIL CHRESTKHA: Yeah, I think
it's somewhat common knowledge

00:08:06.640 --> 00:08:09.580
that data preparation
is still the biggest

00:08:09.580 --> 00:08:13.330
task within a machine
learning pipeline.

00:08:13.330 --> 00:08:17.410
And it's interesting to kind
of hear your story resonate

00:08:17.410 --> 00:08:19.390
with that, that it is
the most demanding and

00:08:19.390 --> 00:08:22.100
resource-intensive part
of the entire pipeline.

00:08:22.100 --> 00:08:27.310
So now, once you have the raw
data processed into TFRecords,

00:08:27.310 --> 00:08:28.090
what happens then?

00:08:28.090 --> 00:08:30.070
Can you walk us through
the training pipeline?

00:08:30.070 --> 00:08:31.028
KSENIA KHLYUSTINA: Yes.

00:08:31.028 --> 00:08:34.659
Then there is another
pipeline that also uses

00:08:34.659 --> 00:08:36.400
Kubeflow as an orchestrator.

00:08:36.400 --> 00:08:41.809
And then it takes TFRecords
generated by previous pipeline.

00:08:41.809 --> 00:08:44.230
It takes TFRecords from
Google Cloud Storage,

00:08:44.230 --> 00:08:50.230
and it takes model source code,
which is just a Python package.

00:08:50.230 --> 00:08:54.490
So it runs in Vertex
AI, and as the output

00:08:54.490 --> 00:08:59.200
it generates a trained model
in the format of TensorFlow

00:08:59.200 --> 00:09:00.370
SavedModel.

00:09:00.370 --> 00:09:04.210
We put it in Google
Cloud Storage as well,

00:09:04.210 --> 00:09:06.910
and register in model
metadata storage.

00:09:06.910 --> 00:09:09.040
So model metadata storage,
I already mentioned it.

00:09:09.040 --> 00:09:11.320
Let me tell you about this.

00:09:11.320 --> 00:09:15.880
This is our internal
tool that we use for,

00:09:15.880 --> 00:09:19.420
as you can guess from the
name, storing model metadata.

00:09:19.420 --> 00:09:28.600
So it has models
with parameters.

00:09:28.600 --> 00:09:32.470
And the parameters usually
include model type,

00:09:32.470 --> 00:09:36.580
model purpose, model
ID, for which client

00:09:36.580 --> 00:09:40.210
this model is being registered,
and on which environment

00:09:40.210 --> 00:09:41.420
we want to use this model.

00:09:41.420 --> 00:09:43.780
So it can be a production
model, integration model,

00:09:43.780 --> 00:09:45.980
stage-in-development
model, and so on.

00:09:45.980 --> 00:09:47.840
And then in this model
metadata storage,

00:09:47.840 --> 00:09:50.620
we also keep history
of our dataset.

00:09:50.620 --> 00:09:55.000
So it also has versions
of data set and their ID.

00:09:55.000 --> 00:09:59.470
So later, you can
just reuse models

00:09:59.470 --> 00:10:02.650
and reuse data sets by just
looking at the model metadata

00:10:02.650 --> 00:10:03.150
storage.

00:10:03.150 --> 00:10:05.108
MIKHAIL CHRESTKHA: And
thanks for sharing that.

00:10:05.108 --> 00:10:07.360
I think model registries
and model metadata storage

00:10:07.360 --> 00:10:09.980
is really a interesting
topic today.

00:10:09.980 --> 00:10:13.480
I think when you start with your
first model or your first five

00:10:13.480 --> 00:10:16.180
models, just storing those
models in a blob storage

00:10:16.180 --> 00:10:16.930
is sufficient.

00:10:16.930 --> 00:10:19.090
But really, when
you scale to tens,

00:10:19.090 --> 00:10:20.770
hundreds of models,
really developing

00:10:20.770 --> 00:10:23.260
a specific workflow
around managing models

00:10:23.260 --> 00:10:25.840
becomes very important
in the ML lifecycle.

00:10:25.840 --> 00:10:27.920
So now that you have
models, of course

00:10:27.920 --> 00:10:30.370
the next important step
is deploying those models

00:10:30.370 --> 00:10:31.240
to run predictions.

00:10:31.240 --> 00:10:33.942
Can you now walk us through the
final pipeline around serving?

00:10:33.942 --> 00:10:34.900
KSENIA KHLYUSTINA: Yes.

00:10:34.900 --> 00:10:38.330
So this is our pipeline that
we call daily inference.

00:10:38.330 --> 00:10:40.120
And this is actually
the pipeline

00:10:40.120 --> 00:10:41.570
that my team is focusing on.

00:10:41.570 --> 00:10:46.570
So this pipeline is
listening to clients' data.

00:10:46.570 --> 00:10:50.530
So it's listening to
Pub/Subs from BigQuery,

00:10:50.530 --> 00:10:55.360
where the client's
data is being dropped.

00:10:55.360 --> 00:10:57.760
After we collect all
the necessary data,

00:10:57.760 --> 00:10:59.620
the pipeline is triggered.

00:10:59.620 --> 00:11:03.070
And as an orchestration, we
use here Argo, not Kubeflow

00:11:03.070 --> 00:11:05.110
as in two previous pipelines.

00:11:05.110 --> 00:11:10.210
So using Argo
Workflow, the pipeline

00:11:10.210 --> 00:11:15.010
is being triggered and
running in Dataflow as well,

00:11:15.010 --> 00:11:17.911
using beam programming model.

00:11:17.911 --> 00:11:23.800
Model inference is pulling
model files from GCP,

00:11:23.800 --> 00:11:28.330
using model metadata storage,
and data from BigQuery.

00:11:28.330 --> 00:11:34.430
So after the
predictor is executed,

00:11:34.430 --> 00:11:38.710
we have predictions in
form of data in BigQuery.

00:11:38.710 --> 00:11:40.750
And then using this
data in BigQuery,

00:11:40.750 --> 00:11:45.250
we can pack these
predictions in whatever

00:11:45.250 --> 00:11:46.880
form our customer needs.

00:11:46.880 --> 00:11:49.840
So for some customers, we
deliver it through the API,

00:11:49.840 --> 00:11:51.850
and for some customers
we deliver in the form

00:11:51.850 --> 00:11:55.120
of text files on SFTP.

00:11:55.120 --> 00:11:57.460
So on this prediction
in BigQuery,

00:11:57.460 --> 00:12:03.640
we also build some metrics which
are later empowering our UI,

00:12:03.640 --> 00:12:07.690
and where analysts
from our airline can go

00:12:07.690 --> 00:12:11.710
and check what's going on
with any specific flight

00:12:11.710 --> 00:12:15.160
and make some decisions if they
want to adjust model outputs

00:12:15.160 --> 00:12:17.310
or not, and if they
want to adjust prices.

00:12:17.310 --> 00:12:21.010
MIKHAIL CHRESTKHA: One follow-up
question-- a lot of enterprises

00:12:21.010 --> 00:12:23.980
are looking to future proof
their platforms for scale.

00:12:23.980 --> 00:12:27.220
So can you talk about maybe how
you've designed this platform

00:12:27.220 --> 00:12:31.570
for the scale you expect as
you onboard new customers, more

00:12:31.570 --> 00:12:32.502
models?

00:12:32.502 --> 00:12:33.460
KSENIA KHLYUSTINA: Yes.

00:12:33.460 --> 00:12:37.450
We are pretty ambitious, and
we want to serve more airlines.

00:12:37.450 --> 00:12:39.460
So, of course,
scaling and onboarding

00:12:39.460 --> 00:12:42.700
new customers is a
big challenge for us.

00:12:42.700 --> 00:12:44.440
But how are we solving this?

00:12:44.440 --> 00:12:49.750
We don't want to be a
professional services company.

00:12:49.750 --> 00:12:54.423
We want to have one generic
solution that fits all.

00:12:54.423 --> 00:12:55.840
Of course, it can
be configurable,

00:12:55.840 --> 00:12:58.610
but it's still one solution.

00:12:58.610 --> 00:13:01.360
And then when we have
every new customer,

00:13:01.360 --> 00:13:04.300
product team-- or our
core engineering team--

00:13:04.300 --> 00:13:05.990
don't need to do anything.

00:13:05.990 --> 00:13:08.463
We already have the solution.

00:13:08.463 --> 00:13:09.880
We already have
all the pipelines.

00:13:09.880 --> 00:13:14.170
So we only need to hire a
customer-success team, which

00:13:14.170 --> 00:13:17.720
needs to take that
data from the customer,

00:13:17.720 --> 00:13:22.510
transform it into our
canonical data model, and then,

00:13:22.510 --> 00:13:24.730
using the pipelines that
are already created,

00:13:24.730 --> 00:13:29.470
start training model on
this specific customer data,

00:13:29.470 --> 00:13:33.700
and then run this model on
our prediction pipeline.

00:13:33.700 --> 00:13:36.490
So, basically, they're empowered
to do everything by themselves,

00:13:36.490 --> 00:13:41.170
and core team can focus
on other features.

00:13:41.170 --> 00:13:43.420
That's how we onboard
new customers.

00:13:43.420 --> 00:13:49.120
It's how we scale in the amount
of the airlines we can onboard.

00:13:49.120 --> 00:13:52.750
But also, this is not the
only challenge with scale.

00:13:52.750 --> 00:13:58.340
And we, of course, want
to broaden our services.

00:13:58.340 --> 00:14:01.390
And we don't want
to only predict

00:14:01.390 --> 00:14:04.040
demand for economy
cabin, for instance.

00:14:04.040 --> 00:14:07.150
We also want to go farther
and predict premium.

00:14:07.150 --> 00:14:11.740
We also want to predict demand
on luggage and the [INAUDIBLE]

00:14:11.740 --> 00:14:14.020
services you can
add to your tickets,

00:14:14.020 --> 00:14:16.190
and then go to cargo, and so on.

00:14:16.190 --> 00:14:20.270
So this, of course, will
result in multiple models.

00:14:20.270 --> 00:14:23.020
So how we will scale this?

00:14:23.020 --> 00:14:26.110
We designed the pipeline
in the way that--

00:14:26.110 --> 00:14:29.140
where we're just able to
add as many models as we

00:14:29.140 --> 00:14:30.740
need and run them in parallel.

00:14:30.740 --> 00:14:33.905
So the only thing
that we will be

00:14:33.905 --> 00:14:36.280
facing during the challenge
we'll be facing is resources.

00:14:36.280 --> 00:14:38.898
But, of course, we can
always buy them from Google.

00:14:38.898 --> 00:14:39.940
MIKHAIL CHRESTKHA: Great.

00:14:39.940 --> 00:14:40.840
Thanks.

00:14:40.840 --> 00:14:44.200
So we all know that ML platforms
are a fast evolving space.

00:14:44.200 --> 00:14:47.380
So curious what future
enhancements you have planned.

00:14:47.380 --> 00:14:49.820
KSENIA KHLYUSTINA:
Yeah, we have a few.

00:14:49.820 --> 00:14:56.170
So first of all, we want to add
feature store to our tooling.

00:14:56.170 --> 00:14:59.080
So this is something
that would allow

00:14:59.080 --> 00:15:03.520
us to version our features
to just better manage them.

00:15:03.520 --> 00:15:09.760
And then also, something that
would let us not generate them

00:15:09.760 --> 00:15:11.890
on daily basis but only append.

00:15:11.890 --> 00:15:16.300
Because historical data from
the airlines, it doesn't change.

00:15:16.300 --> 00:15:18.740
We only get a new
portion of data every day

00:15:18.740 --> 00:15:20.320
and for yesterday.

00:15:20.320 --> 00:15:24.670
But, yeah, we don't need to
regenerate the entire history

00:15:24.670 --> 00:15:26.778
to be able to run new inference.

00:15:26.778 --> 00:15:28.570
So this is something
we've been working on.

00:15:28.570 --> 00:15:30.430
And apart from
that, we also want

00:15:30.430 --> 00:15:33.670
to be able to do
dynamic pricing.

00:15:33.670 --> 00:15:36.370
And for that we will need
to add some online modelling

00:15:36.370 --> 00:15:38.170
to our inference pipeline.

00:15:38.170 --> 00:15:44.240
So we want to be able to
receive a request from customer

00:15:44.240 --> 00:15:46.910
and then be able to
respond immediately

00:15:46.910 --> 00:15:48.500
with an optimal price.

00:15:48.500 --> 00:15:48.740
MIKHAIL CHRESTKHA: Great.

00:15:48.740 --> 00:15:50.115
Yeah, thank you--
really exciting

00:15:50.115 --> 00:15:53.330
roadmap, both from a
new business capability

00:15:53.330 --> 00:15:56.480
standpoint as well as the
technology capabilities

00:15:56.480 --> 00:15:57.620
of the ML platform.

00:15:57.620 --> 00:15:59.870
So thank you, Ksenia, for
joining us today and sharing

00:15:59.870 --> 00:16:02.600
what FLYR Labs does and how
you've architected an ML

00:16:02.600 --> 00:16:04.598
platform on GCP.

00:16:04.598 --> 00:16:06.140
KSENIA KHLYUSTINA:
Thank you so much.

00:16:06.140 --> 00:16:07.790
MIKHAIL CHRESTKHA: Yeah, really
had a great conversation.

00:16:07.790 --> 00:16:10.400
It's especially important that
everyone learn from each other,

00:16:10.400 --> 00:16:13.525
as MLOps and ML platforms
are still a fairly new field.

00:16:13.525 --> 00:16:14.900
I personally
learned a lot, and I

00:16:14.900 --> 00:16:16.610
know many others will as well.

00:16:16.610 --> 00:16:18.140
Everyone, if you
like this content,

00:16:18.140 --> 00:16:19.890
please like and subscribe
